name: first steps
boss: n00b
flavor: You don't know where you are yet.
brief: Three commands answer "where am I, and what is here". pwd prints the
brief: directory you are standing in. ls lists what it contains. cd moves you.
brief: Everything else in this system is those three plus a filter.
brief:
brief: Answers RUN. Type what you would really type -- if it produces the right
brief: output it is right, whether or not it is the phrasing anyone expected.
---
q: {print|show} the directory you are currently in
a: pwd
out: ^/
ref: pwd
teach: pwd
hint: print working directory
---
q: {list|show} the contents of the current directory
a: ls
out: server\.log
ref: ls
teach: ls
hint: two letters
---
q: list the current directory, including names that start with a dot
a: ls -a
out: ^\.$|^\.\.$
ref: ls
teach: ls -a
hint: the flag ls describes as "Include names starting with ."
---
q: list @dir@ in long format, so you can see sizes and permissions
a: ls -l @dir@
a: ls -l @dir@/
out: ^total
ref: ls
teach: ls -l
hint: the long format flag, then the path
---
q: list the current directory with sizes in K and M rather than raw bytes
a: ls -lh
a: ls -hl
out: ^total
ref: ls
teach: ls -h
hint: two flags -- long format, and the one ls calls "Human readable"
---
q: list the current directory {newest first|sorted by modification time}
a: ls -lt
a: ls -t
out: .
ref: ls
teach: ls -t
hint: sort by time
---
q: list the current directory one name per line, nothing else
a: ls -1
out: ^server\.log$
ref: ls
teach: ls -1
hint: the digit one, not the letter l
---
q: {print|echo} the word @word@
a: echo @word@
out: ^@word@$
ref: echo
teach: echo
---
q: print @word@ with no trailing newline
a: echo -n @word@
out: @word@
ref: echo
teach: echo -n
hint: n for newline -- the flag suppresses it
---
q: print the value of your HOME directory using the HOME variable
a: echo $HOME
a: echo "$HOME"
a: echo ${HOME}
out: ^/
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: a dollar sign in front of the name
---
q: change into @dir@
a: cd @dir@
a: cd @dir@/
a: cd ./@dir@
ref: cd
teach: cd
---
q: go up one directory
a: cd ..
a: cd ../
ref: cd
teach: cd
hint: two dots mean "the parent"
---
q: go back to your home directory, in the shortest way possible
a: cd
ref: cd
teach: cd
hint: cd with no argument already means home
---
q: list the current directory showing hidden names but not . and ..
a: ls -A
out: .
ref: ls
teach: ls -A
---
q: list @dir@ itself rather than what is inside it
a: ls -d @dir@
out: ^@dir@$
ref: ls
teach: ls -d
---
q: list the current directory marking directories with a slash and executables with a star
a: ls -F
out: /
ref: ls
teach: ls -F
---
q: list the current directory showing each file's inode number
a: ls -i
out: ^[0-9]
ref: ls
teach: ls -i
---
q: list in long format showing numeric user and group ids rather than names
a: ls -ln
out: ^total
ref: ls
teach: ls -n
---
q: list marking only directories with a trailing slash
a: ls -p
out: /
ref: ls
teach: ls -p
---
q: list the current directory in reverse order
a: ls -r
out: .
ref: ls
teach: ls -r
---
q: list everything under @dir@ recursively
a: ls -R @dir@
out: .
ref: ls
teach: ls -R
---
q: list the current directory largest file first
a: ls -S
out: .
ref: ls
teach: ls -S
---
q: list showing the block size each file occupies
a: ls -s
out: [0-9]
ref: ls
teach: ls -s
---
q: list the current directory across the screen in columns
a: ls -x
out: .
ref: ls
teach: ls -x
